{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 15, :comments ["<b>And they shall not profane the holy offerings of the children of Israel</b> — by feeding them to strangers. <b>And they shall bear them the iniquity of guilt</b> — they shall load upon themselves iniquity for eating the sacred donations that were set apart for the name of terumah and became sanctified and forbidden to them. And Onkelos, who translated 'בְּמֵיכַלְהוֹן בְּסוֹאֲבָא' (by feeding them to one who is unfit), did not translate so for no reason — this is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Leviticus 22:16), of blessed memory. But I do not understand his intent, for it appears from his words that this negative commandment is an admonition to the priests, that they shall not feed terumah to strangers, and if so, it would be fitting to explain that 'and they shall bear them' refers to Israel, that the priests would load upon Israel the iniquity of guilt when they eat the sacred donations — so why did Rabbi Yishmael need to derive it from the word itself (Sifrei Naso 32)? And furthermore, this negative commandment is not mentioned in the Talmud; rather, it is prohibited to do so as with all other prohibitions. But the correct sense of the verse is that 'the children of Israel' connects to what precedes: 'and the children of Israel shall not profane the holy offerings of the children of Israel which they lift up to HaShem,' and this is a second admonition concerning the stranger, that he shall not eat holy food, because Scripture wished to mention in it the punishment. And it says, 'and they shall bear themselves iniquity,' etc., when they eat their holy offerings. And our Sages derived from the redundancy of 'one who eats' concerning tevel (untithed produce): they said (Sanhedrin 83a), from where do we learn that one who eats tevel is liable to death, as it is written, 'and they shall not profane the holy offerings of the children of Israel which they lift up to HaShem' — Scripture speaks of that which they will tithe in the future, and they learn ḥillul, ḥillul from terumah. But in Torat Kohanim (chapter 6:8–10) I found: 'and they shall not profane' — to include the anointing and the drinking. 'The holy offerings of the children of Israel' — for the holy offerings of Israel one is liable for principal and a fifth, but for the holy offerings of gentiles one is not liable for principal and a fifth. One might think they are liable for terumah that is in tevel — Scripture says, 'which they lift up to HaShem' — for what has been separated they are liable, but they are not liable for the terumah that is in tevel. 'And they shall bear them the iniquity of guilt' — this teaches that even for tevel they are liable to death. And this is not in the manner of the Gemara; rather, I say that the inclusion of anointing and drinking is an asmakhta (a scriptural support for a rabbinic law), for drinking is included in eating and anointing is rabbinic, as is explicitly stated in the Jerusalem Talmud (Ma'aser Sheni 2:1). And because concerning tevel Scripture says, 'and they shall not profane the holy offerings of the children of Israel,' because they are destined to separate from it for HaShem, they derived from this that what has been separated is what is called kodesh, not the tevel. If so, it is not included in 'when he eats holy food unwittingly' (Leviticus 22:14), for which one is liable to a fifth. Therefore Scripture repeated, 'and they shall bear them the iniquity of guilt,' teaching that even for tevel they are liable to death, for it is the terumah within tevel that is referred to here. And the general principle is that this verse concerns tevel: first it admonished and punished concerning the separated kodesh, and then it admonished concerning what they will separate in the future, according to the midrash of our Sages."], :book :leviticus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 22, :notes {0 "The abbreviations ת\"כ and בת\"כ both refer to Torat Kohanim (Sifra); resolved as Torat Kohanim based on context."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:58:06.580882720Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}